  "content": {
    "id": "p35-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Consider the parabola $y=x^2$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p35-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write the equation of $g(x)=4f(x)$. What dilation is this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$g(x)=4x^2$. Vertical dilation by factor $4$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=4f(x)\\\\g(x)&=4x^2\\end{align*}$$All $y$ coordinates are multiplied by $4$: a vertical dilation by factor $4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Write the equation of $h(x)=f(2x)$. What dilation is this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(x)=4x^2$. Horizontal dilation by factor $\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}h(x)&=f(2x)\\\\h(x)&=(2x)^2\\\\h(x)&=4x^2\\end{align*}$$Replacing $x$ with $2x$ multiplies all $x$ coordinates by $\\frac12$: a horizontal dilation by factor $\\frac12$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What do you notice about the answers to parts a and b?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both give the same equation $y=4x^2$. For $y=x^2$, a vertical dilation by factor $k^2$ is equivalent to a horizontal dilation by factor $\\frac1k$, where $k>0$.",
          "worked": "Both give the same equation $y=4x^2$. More generally, for $k>0$,$$\\begin{align*}f(kx)&=(kx)^2\\\\f(kx)&=k^2x^2\\\\f(kx)&=k^2f(x)\\end{align*}$$Thus a horizontal dilation by factor $\\frac1k$ gives the same graph as a vertical dilation by factor $k^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
